{"maxId":293,"entries":{"copy|Pressure injuries":["u1"],"depth|Staging, as a depth":["u2"],"staging-npiap|Staging — NPIAP":["u3"],"c46-staging-table|row:Stage 1|TH|Stage 1":["u4"],"c46-staging-table|row:Stage 1|TD|Intact skin with a localized area of non-blanchable erythema, which may appear differently in darkly pigmented skin. Blanchable erythema or changes in sensation, temperature, or firmness may precede visual changes. Color changes do not include purple or maroon discoloration; these may indicate deep tissue pressure injury.":["u5"],"c46-staging-table|row:Stage 2|TH|Stage 2":["u6"],"c46-staging-table|row:Stage 2|TD|Partial-thickness loss of skin with exposed dermis. The wound bed is viable, pink or red, and moist; it may present as an intact or ruptured serum-filled blister. Adipose (fat) and deeper tissues are not visible. Granulation tissue, slough, and eschar are not present. These injuries commonly result from adverse microclimate and shear in the skin over the pelvis, and shear in the heel. This stage must not be used for moisture-associated skin damage, including incontinence-associated dermatitis and intertriginous dermatitis; medical adhesive-related skin injury; or traumatic wounds such as skin tears, burns, and abrasions.":["u7"],"c46-staging-table|row:Stage 3|TH|Stage 3":["u8"],"c46-staging-table|row:Stage 3|TD|Full-thickness loss of skin, with adipose (fat) visible in the ulcer. Granulation tissue and epibole (rolled wound edges) are often present. Slough and/or eschar may be visible. The depth of tissue damage varies by anatomical location; areas of significant adiposity can develop deep wounds. Undermining and tunneling may occur. Fascia, muscle, tendon, ligament, cartilage, and bone are not exposed. If slough or eschar obscures the extent of tissue loss, this is an unstageable pressure injury.":["u9"],"c46-staging-table|row:Stage 4|TH|Stage 4":["u10"],"c46-staging-table|row:Stage 4|TD|Full-thickness skin and tissue loss with exposed or directly palpable fascia, muscle, tendon, ligament, cartilage, or bone in the ulcer. Slough and/or eschar may be visible. Epibole (rolled edges), undermining, and/or tunneling often occur. Depth varies by anatomical location. If slough or eschar obscures the extent of tissue loss, this is an unstageable pressure injury.":["u11"],"c46-staging-table|row:Unstageable|TH|Unstageable":["u12"],"c46-staging-table|row:Unstageable|TD|Full-thickness skin and tissue loss in which the extent of tissue damage within the ulcer cannot be confirmed because it is obscured by slough or eschar. If slough or eschar is removed, a stage 3 or stage 4 pressure injury will be revealed. Stable eschar (dry, adherent, intact, without erythema or fluctuance) on an ischemic limb or the heel should not be removed.":["u13"],"c46-staging-table|row:Deep tissue pressure injury|TH|Deep tissue pressure injury":["u14"],"c46-staging-table|row:Deep tissue pressure injury|TD|Intact or nonintact skin with a localized area of persistent nonblanchable deep red, maroon, or purple discoloration, or epidermal separation revealing a dark wound bed or blood-filled blister. Pain and temperature change often precede skin color changes. Discoloration may appear differently in darkly pigmented skin. This injury results from intense and/or prolonged pressure and shear forces at the bone-muscle interface. The wound may evolve rapidly to reveal the actual extent of tissue injury or may resolve without tissue loss. If necrotic tissue, subcutaneous tissue, granulation tissue, fascia, muscle, or other underlying structures are visible, this indicates a full-thickness pressure injury (unstageable, stage 3, or stage 4). Do not use deep tissue pressure injury to describe vascular, traumatic, neuropathic, or dermatologic conditions.":["u15"],"c46-device-related|row:Device-related|TH|Device-related":["u16"],"c46-device-related|row:Device-related|TD|Conforms to the shape of the device. Staged as above":["u17"],"copy|row:Mucosal membrane|TH|Mucosal membrane":["u18"],"copy|row:Mucosal membrane|TD|From a device at a mucosal site. Cannot be staged — mucosa has no keratinised layer":["u19"],"c46-staging-npiap-2|Depth of tissue loss by stage. Stage 1 is intact skin with non-blanchable erythema; stage 2 reaches the dermis; stage 3 exposes subcutaneous fat; stage 4 exposes fascia, muscle, tendon, cartilage or bone. Deep tissue injury is a persistent deep red, maroon or purple discolouration, or a blood blister, with the damage beneath intact or broken skin. Drawn to the NPIAP definitions used in ch 46.":["u20"],"c46-staging-npiap-3|Stage 1 blanches? Then it is reactive hyperaemia, not a pressure injury. The test is whether it fails to blanch. In darker skin the colour change is unreliable — use temperature, firmness and pain instead":["u21"],"c46-staging-npiap-4|Stage 2 has no slough and no granulation. If either is present it is deeper than stage 2 by definition, whatever it looks like":["u22"],"copy|Deep tissue injury reflects intense pressure about 48 hours earlier, and muscle injury may precede visible skin damage. Stage 1 changes are reversible but may take 1 to 3 weeks to settle; stage 2 heals in 2 to 4 weeks":["u128"],"c46-staging-npiap-6|Deep tissue injury can declare either way — it may resolve without breaking, or open into a full-thickness injury within days. Documenting it early protects the patient and you":["u24"],"copy|Mucosal injuries cannot be staged at all, because mucosa has no keratinised layer to stage through":["u25"],"how-common-and-what-they-cost|How common, and what they cost":["u26"],"c46-how-common-and-what-they-cost-1|About 70% of pressure injuries occur in people over 65, in 9–22% of nursing home residents and 5–32% of hospital patients":["u27"],"copy|Healing is slow and stage-dependent: 75% of stage 2 wounds heal within 60 days, against only 17% of stage 3 or 4. A full-thickness injury should at least show improvement every 2 to 4 weeks; lack of progress calls for reassessment of pressure relief, infection and the wound-care plan.":["u186"],"copy|Osteomyelitis is reported in 32% of stage 4 injuries — the strongest clinical indicator is palpable soft or rough bone, or visible bone.":["u187"],"copy|60% of chronic wounds contain a biofilm; control combines appropriate debridement with topical therapy, with maintenance treatment because biofilm can re-form.":["u188"],"where-they-form-and-what-the-definition-actually-says|Where they form, and what the definition actually says":["u31"],"c46-where-they-form-and-what-the-definition-actually-says-1|The sites: sacrum, heels, ischial tuberosities, trochanters, lateral malleoli — sacrum and heels commonest. But a pressure injury can form on any soft tissue under pressure, so do not let location alone decide the aetiology":["u32"],"c46-where-they-form-and-what-the-definition-actually-says-2|The NPIAP modifiers: tissue tolerance for pressure and shear is altered by microclimate — the heat and moisture at the skin surface — and by nutrition, perfusion, comorbidities and the condition of the soft tissue itself. That list is why two patients on the same mattress for the same hours do not fare the same":["u33"],"c46-where-they-form-and-what-the-definition-actually-says-3|The name matters. Pressure ulcer, bedsore and decubitus ulcer all imply confinement to bed; these injuries form in chairs and under devices too, so pressure injury is the term the chapter uses throughout":["u34"],"pi-mechanics|How pressure injures tissue":["u129"],"copy|row:Preparation|TH|Preparation":["u130"],"copy|row:Preparation|TH|Exposure":["u131"],"copy|row:Preparation|TH|Reported damage":["u132"],"c46-strain-single-cell|row:Single muscle cells|TH|Single muscle cells":["u133"],"c46-strain-single-cell|row:Single muscle cells|TD|Deformation exceeding 80%.":["u134"],"c46-strain-single-cell|row:Single muscle cells|TD|Consistent membrane rupture with immediate, irreversible damage.":["u135"],"c46-strain-whole-muscle|row:Whole muscle|TH|Whole muscle":["u136"],"c46-strain-whole-muscle|row:Whole muscle|TD|Strain higher than 50%, sustained for 2 hours.":["u137"],"c46-strain-whole-muscle|row:Whole muscle|TD|Irreversible muscle injury.":["u138"],"copy|row:Force|TH|Force":["u139"],"copy|row:Force|TH|Direction and tissue effect":["u140"],"copy|row:Force|TH|Relationship to pressure injury":["u141"],"c46-force-pressure|row:Pressure|TH|Pressure":["u142"],"c46-force-pressure|row:Pressure|TD|Perpendicular loading compresses and deforms tissue between bone and the support surface.":["u143"],"c46-force-pressure|row:Pressure|TD|Direct deformation and impaired perfusion can injure deep tissue.":["u144"],"c46-force-shear|row:Shear|TH|Shear":["u145"],"c46-force-shear|row:Shear|TD|Parallel force produces shear strain: tissue distortion, cell-membrane deformation, stretching and lateral displacement of vessels.":["u146"],"c46-force-shear|row:Shear|TD|Can cause tissue ischaemia and contribute to pressure injury.":["u147"],"c46-force-friction|row:Friction|TH|Friction":["u148"],"c46-force-friction|row:Friction|TD|Superficial force damages upper skin layers and reduces tolerance to other forces.":["u149"],"c46-force-friction|row:Friction|TD|Not considered a true cause of pressure injury. Chronic friction-related hyperkeratosis and hyperpigmentation are not classified as pressure injury.":["u150"],"risk-assessment|Risk assessment":["u35"],"prevention|Prevention":["u36"],"copy|Residents were placed on high-density foam mattresses and randomly assigned to turning schedules of every 2, 3, or 4 hours in the chapter's trial that found no significant difference in pressure-injury incidence — 942 residents, and the rates were 2.5% at two-hourly, 0.6% at three-hourly and 3.1% at four-hourly, with no significant difference between them. For side-lying, position the patient at a 30-degree angle to the support surface, with the upper leg forward of the lower leg and a pillow separating the legs. Keep the head of the bed at the lowest elevation consistent with the patient's medical condition, and limit the time it is elevated.":["u285"],"copy|Seated patients need weight shifted every 15 minutes where they can do it themselves, and repositioning at least hourly in a chair — sitting concentrates more pressure over a smaller area than lying does":["u38"],"c46-prevention-2|Prophylactic multilayer foam at the sacrum and heels in critical care cut injuries at the heel (3% vs 12%) and sacrum (1% vs 5%), number needed to treat 10":["u39"],"c46-prevention-3|Offload heels completely, floating them off the bed; heel devices rather than pillows that slip":["u40"],"c46-prevention-4|Support surfaces — reactive or alternating-pressure, selected by risk and by whether the patient can reposition themselves":["u41"],"c46-prevention-5|Skin and moisture — incontinence-associated dermatitis is a separate diagnosis from a pressure injury and is treated with barrier products, not repositioning":["u42"],"c46-prevention-6|Nutrition — adequate protein and calories. Routine high-dose vitamin C or zinc is not supported in the absence of deficiency":["u43"],"c46-prevention-7|Prophylactic multilayer foam dressings over sacrum and heels in high-risk patients":["u44"],"pi-support-surfaces|Support surfaces and operative positioning":["u151"],"pi-pain-burden|Pain: frequency, severity and undertreatment":["u189"],"dressings-chosen-by-the-wound|Dressings, chosen by the wound":["u45"],"copy|row:Wound|TH|Wound":["u46"],"copy|row:Wound|TH|Choice":["u47"],"c46-dressings-chosen-by-the-wound-1|row:undefined|TD|Hydrogels (TABLE 46-2)":["u286"],"c46-dressings-chosen-by-the-wound-1|row:undefined|TD|Absorbs low to moderate drainage; Autolysis; Nonadhesive, may have adhesive borders; Semipermeable or impermeable. Notes: Can dry out; May macerate surrounding tissues; Requires secondary dressing or tape to keep in place; Does not cause reinjury upon removal.":["u287"],"c46-transparent-film|row:undefined|TD|Transparent Film Dressings (TABLE 46-2)":["u288"],"c46-transparent-film|row:undefined|TD|Polyurethane and polyethylene membrane film coated with a layer of acrylic hypoallergenic adhesive; Appropriate for partial-thickness wounds; Promotes epithelialization; Semipermeable. Notes: May reinjure area on removal; Nonabsorbent so can lead to wound edge maceration; Tendency to remove prematurely.":["u289"],"c46-dressings-chosen-by-the-wound-2|row:undefined|TD|Moderate to heavy exudate":["u50"],"c46-dressings-chosen-by-the-wound-2|row:undefined|TD|Foam, alginate or hydrofibre — absorbs":["u51"],"c46-dressings-chosen-by-the-wound-3|row:undefined|TD|Painful wound":["u52"],"c46-dressings-chosen-by-the-wound-3|row:undefined|TD|Hydrogel, hydrocolloid, or soft-silicone non-adherent contact layers. Asked in 2026: the dressing that relieves pain is the one that keeps the bed moist and does not adhere on removal. Avoid gauze that dries onto the surface":["u53"],"c46-dressings-chosen-by-the-wound-4|row:undefined|TD|Cavity, undermining":["u54"],"c46-dressings-chosen-by-the-wound-4|row:undefined|TD|Loosely packed filler; never tight packing":["u55"],"c46-dressings-chosen-by-the-wound-5|row:undefined|TD|Critical colonisation":["u56"],"c46-dressings-chosen-by-the-wound-5|row:undefined|TD|Short-course topical antiseptic or silver; reassess at two weeks":["u57"],"pi-biofilm|Colonisation, inflammation and biofilm":["u190"],"copy|row:Tissue / wound context|TH|Tissue / wound context":["u191"],"copy|row:Tissue / wound context|TH|Meaning of the chapter’s ≥10^5 organisms/mL comparison":["u192"],"c46-bioburden-intact|row:Intact skin|TH|Intact skin":["u193"],"c46-bioburden-intact|row:Intact skin|TD|At this level, microbial growth can cause local infection.":["u194"],"c46-bioburden-surgical|row:Surgical wounds|TH|Surgical wounds":["u195"],"c46-bioburden-surgical|row:Surgical wounds|TD|At this level, microbial growth can impair healing.":["u196"],"c46-bioburden-chronic|row:Chronic pressure injuries|TH|Chronic pressure injuries":["u197"],"c46-bioburden-chronic|row:Chronic pressure injuries|TD|May sustain growth at this level for prolonged periods without noticeable clinical infection and with some healing. The number alone does not establish infection.":["u198"],"copy|row:Context|TH|Context":["u199"],"copy|row:Context|TH|Role of matrix metalloproteinases":["u200"],"c46-mmp-normal|row:Normal wound healing|TH|Normal wound healing":["u201"],"c46-mmp-normal|row:Normal wound healing|TD|Matrix modification supports cell migration and tissue remodelling throughout healing.":["u202"],"c46-mmp-excess|row:Chronic wound: excessive expression|TH|Chronic wound: excessive expression":["u203"],"c46-mmp-excess|row:Chronic wound: excessive expression|TD|Excess matrix degradation retards healing and promotes ongoing inflammation.":["u204"],"pi-infection-assessment|Infection: clinical assessment and the right sample":["u205"],"pi-bone-infection|Osteomyelitis and faecal contamination":["u206"],"pi-surgical-outcomes|Surgical outcomes: keep the population and endpoint attached":["u207"],"copy|row:Population|TH|Population":["u208"],"copy|row:Population|TH|Reported same-site recurrence":["u209"],"c46-recurrence-older|row:Older patients|TH|Older patients":["u210"],"c46-recurrence-older|row:Older patients|TD|40%.":["u211"],"c46-recurrence-younger|row:Younger paraplegic patients|TH|Younger paraplegic patients":["u212"],"c46-recurrence-younger|row:Younger paraplegic patients|TD|More than 70%.":["u213"],"debridement-and-infection|Debridement and infection":["u58"],"c46-debridement-and-infection-2|Sharp, enzymatic, autolytic or larval, according to urgency and setting":["u59"],"c46-debridement-and-infection-3|Stable eschar (dry, adherent, intact, without erythema or fluctuance) on an ischemic limb or the heel should not be removed.":["u60"],"c46-debridement-and-infection-4|Assess infection clinically and choose the specimen accordingly. Surface or drainage culture alone does not establish tissue infection; tissue biopsy or a quantitative wound-bed swab can assess wound bioburden.":["u214"],"c46-debridement-and-infection-5|Palpable soft or rough bone, or visible bone, should trigger evaluation for osteomyelitis. Confirmation and a treatment plan are needed; neither a positive surface swab nor another empirical antibiotic course substitutes for that evaluation.":["u215"],"when-healing-is-not-the-goal|When healing is not the goal":["u63"],"nutrition-and-what-the-evidence-actually-supports|Nutrition, and what the evidence actually supports":["u64"],"the-ones-that-are-not-pressure-injuries|The ones that are not pressure injuries":["u65"],"copy|row:Looks like|TH|Looks like":["u66"],"copy|row:Looks like|TH|Actually":["u67"],"copy|row:Looks like|TH|Giveaway":["u68"],"c46-the-ones-that-are-not-pressure-injuries-1|row:undefined|TD|Stage 2 over the sacrum":["u69"],"c46-the-ones-that-are-not-pressure-injuries-1|row:undefined|TD|Incontinence-associated dermatitis":["u70"],"c46-the-ones-that-are-not-pressure-injuries-1|row:undefined|TD|Diffuse, in the skin folds rather than over the bone, no depth; treated with barrier products, not repositioning":["u71"],"c46-heel-differential-presentation|row:undefined|TD|Unstageable heel wound":["u72"],"c46-heel-differential-diagnosis|row:undefined|TD|Arterial ulcer":["u73"],"copy|row:undefined|TD|Absent pulses, punched-out, painful, dry base; assess perfusion before debriding anything":["u74"],"c46-terminal-ulcers-table|row:undefined|TD|Deep tissue injury":["u75"],"c46-terminal-ulcers-table|row:undefined|TD|Skin failure and terminal ulcers":["u76"],"c46-terminal-ulcers-table|row:undefined|TD|The wounds described as skin failure or terminal ulcers are not large enough to lead to death on their own, such as would be seen with epidermolysis bullosa or Stevens-Johnson syndrome. The chapter states that the condition needs a different name.":["u290"],"c46-the-ones-that-are-not-pressure-injuries-3|row:undefined|TD|Chronic non-healing edge":["u78"],"c46-the-ones-that-are-not-pressure-injuries-3|row:undefined|TD|Malignant change, or osteomyelitis":["u79"],"c46-the-ones-that-are-not-pressure-injuries-3|row:undefined|TD|Rolled edges, or probes to bone; biopsy or MRI, not another dressing":["u80"],"key-clinical-points-ch-46|Key Clinical Points — ch 46":["u81"],"copy|row:#|TH|#":["u82"],"copy|row:#|TH|Point":["u83"],"c46-key-clinical-points-ch-46-1|row:undefined|TD|1":["u84"],"c46-key-clinical-points-ch-46-1|row:undefined|TD|Pressure injuries arise from mechanical force compressing tissue between the bony skeleton and an external surface, directly damaging the cell wall and leading to cell death, plus ischaemia from occluded capillaries and lymphatics and reperfusion injury from oxygen free radicals.":["u85"],"c46-key-clinical-points-ch-46-2|row:undefined|TD|2":["u86"],"c46-key-clinical-points-ch-46-2|row:undefined|TD|Prevention = screening for risk, followed by standardised risk assessment to determine individual-specific risk, followed by targeted prevention interventions.":["u87"],"c46-key-clinical-points-ch-46-3|row:undefined|TD|3":["u88"],"c46-key-clinical-points-ch-46-3|row:undefined|TD|Key prevention strategies: scheduled repositioning programmes, reactive and active support surfaces, assessment and management of nutrition, and prophylactic dressings.":["u89"],"c46-key-clinical-points-ch-46-4|row:undefined|TD|4":["u90"],"c46-key-clinical-points-ch-46-4|row:undefined|TD|Appropriate care rests on adequate, timely and complete debridement of necrotic tissue, identification and treatment of infection and biofilm, and a moist wound environment.":["u91"],"c46-key-clinical-points-ch-46-5|row:undefined|TD|5":["u92"],"c46-key-clinical-points-ch-46-5|row:undefined|TD|Documentation must include pressure injury risk status, prevention strategies, assessment (size, stage, location and wound-bed description at minimum), treatment plan, and evaluation of treatment success.":["u93"],"c46-key-clinical-points-ch-46-6|row:undefined|TD|6":["u94"],"c46-key-clinical-points-ch-46-6|row:undefined|TD|Healing benchmarks: partial-thickness (stage 2) injuries should heal within 60 days maximum; full-thickness (stage 3/4/unstageable) injuries should show improvement every 2 to 4 weeks.":["u95"],"pi-quality-burden|Hospital burden and the quality agenda":["u278"],"copy|CHAPTER 46 / HAZZARD":["u96"],"c46-definition-1|A pressure injury is localised damage to skin and underlying soft tissue, usually over a bony prominence or related to a medical or other device, caused by intense or prolonged pressure, or pressure combined with shear.":["u97"],"c46-definition-1|It may be intact skin or an open wound, and it may be painful.":["u98"],"c46-depth-1|The staging system is not a severity scale — it is a statement about which tissue plane has been lost.":["u99"],"c46-depth-1|Reading it that way makes the whole table fall out, including why you cannot reverse-stage and why an obscured base cannot be staged at all.":["u100"],"c46-staging-npiap-1|Do not reverse-stage.":["u101"],"c46-staging-npiap-1|A healing stage 4 does not become a stage 2 — lost tissue is replaced by scar, not by the original structures.":["u102"],"c46-staging-npiap-1|Describe it as a healing stage 4.":["u103"],"c46-how-common-and-what-they-cost-5|Pressure-injury pain is common at rest and during dressing changes, yet often undertreated.":["u216"],"c46-how-common-and-what-they-cost-5|The highest pain category reported by 18% applies to those with dressing-change pain.":["u217"],"c46-how-common-and-what-they-cost-5|The chapter advises analgesia 30 minutes before a painful procedure.":["u218"],"c46-mechanical-loading|Pressure is a perpendicular load over an area.":["u152"],"c46-mechanical-loading|Gravity loads the skeleton and deforms the soft tissue trapped between a bony prominence and its support surface.":["u153"],"c46-mechanical-loading|Less compressible tissue between bone and surface produces a steeper pressure gradient, helping explain injury over bony prominences.":["u154"],"c46-deformation-initiation|High-magnitude loading can directly deform or destroy muscle-cell membranes—for example after prolonged lying on the floor with a fractured hip.":["u155"],"c46-deformation-initiation|Tissue deformation is central to deep tissue injury and full-thickness stage 3 and 4 injuries and may initiate cell death.":["u156"],"c46-deformation-susceptibility|The degree of deformation depends on tissue-layer geometry, stiffness and strength, bone shape, and the magnitude and distribution of external force.":["u157"],"c46-deformation-susceptibility|Tolerance changes with age, lifestyle, injury and disease.":["u158"],"c46-strain-scope|These observations concern different experimental preparations and exposure durations.":["u159"],"c46-strain-scope|They are not interchangeable thresholds or bedside limits for safe pressure exposure.":["u160"],"c46-deep-muscle-injury|Ischaemic muscle is particularly vulnerable to deformation because it is metabolically active.":["u161"],"c46-deep-muscle-injury|Irreversible muscle injury may occur without equivalent damage in the skin or subcutaneous layers.":["u162"],"c46-deep-muscle-injury|Cell death and local necrosis then change tissue geometry, increasing deformation and amplifying injury.":["u163"],"c46-heat-inflammation|Heat accumulation raises metabolic rate and oxygen demand, worsening ischaemia and hypoxia.":["u164"],"c46-heat-inflammation|Inflammation increases interstitial pressure and impedes arteriolar flow; capillary collapse and thrombosis follow, while reduced lymphatic drainage promotes oedema and necrosis.":["u165"],"c46-position-pressure-sites|High-pressure sites vary with position: occiput, sacrum and heels when semi-reclined; ischial tuberosities in erect sitting; and trochanters in side-lying.":["u166"],"c46-chronic-friction|Repeated sliding from a chair to stand or moving between surfaces can produce chronic friction injury.":["u167"],"c46-chronic-friction|Its thickened, often hyperpigmented skin should not be relabelled as a pressure injury.":["u168"],"c46-friction-interface-premise|Topical preparations or suitable fabrics and linens, including silk or noncotton blends, can help reduce friction-related injury by reducing resistance at the skin–linen or skin–support interface—the chapter describes reducing surface tension between them.":["u282"],"c46-friction-moving-conclusion|Appropriate moving technique also lessens friction-induced skin breakdown.":["u283"],"c46-friction-moving-conclusion|Reducing friction at the interface complements careful movement; friction-related superficial injury remains distinct from a true pressure injury.":["u284"],"c46-nutrition-body-habitus|Protein-calorie malnutrition with low BMI reduces padding over bony prominences.":["u169"],"c46-nutrition-body-habitus|Undernourished patients also have poorer functional status, longer ICU stays, greater acuity and comorbidity, and higher mortality.":["u170"],"c46-nutrition-body-habitus|Obesity also increases risk; abundant body mass is not assurance of tissue protection.":["u171"],"c46-scar-strength|The chapter reports scar tensile strength at 40% of native tissue.":["u172"],"c46-scar-strength|A healed stage 4 sacral injury therefore leaves a site vulnerable to future injury rather than restoring the original tissue’s strength.":["u173"],"c46-four-pathophysiology-hypotheses|The chapter lists four mechanisms: (1) direct cellular deformation under high-magnitude pressure; (2) ischaemia from capillary occlusion; (3) shear-induced tissue ischaemia; and (4) reduced pressure tolerance from conditions such as malnutrition, arterial disease, neuropathy, moisture exposure or previous full-thickness injury.":["u174"],"c46-risk-assessment-1|If the wound base cannot be seen, no stage can be assigned — and that is the correct answer, not a cop-out.":["u106"],"c46-risk-assessment-1|“Unstageable” is a finding.":["u107"],"copy|Braden is the instrument to know: six subscales — sensory perception, moisture, activity, mobility, nutrition, and friction and shear.":["u108"],"copy|The first five score 1–4, friction and shear scores 1–3, giving 6–23, with lower being worse and 18 or below commonly taken as at risk.":["u109"],"copy|Norton is the older alternative.":["u110"],"copy|Clinical judgement plus a documented scale beats either alone.":["u111"],"c46-institutional-mattress|Most institutions that care for patients or residents use a 4-inch viscoelastic foam mattress on the beds.":["u291"],"c46-surface-redistribution|Spreading load over a larger area lowers the relative pressure at any one point.":["u175"],"c46-surface-redistribution|The chapter’s analogy is a hand pressed onto many nails rather than one nail: redistribution changes how concentrated the force is.":["u176"],"c46-reactive-surfaces|Reactive surfaces may be powered or nonpowered, but change load distribution only in response to an applied load.":["u177"],"c46-reactive-surfaces|Immersion and envelopment spread the body’s load; a foam mattress is a nonpowered reactive surface.":["u178"],"c46-active-surfaces|Active surfaces are powered: air cells inflate and deflate to shift the supported areas periodically, avoiding sustained deformation at one site.":["u179"],"c46-active-surfaces|An alternating-air mattress is an active surface.":["u180"],"c46-active-surfaces|The chapter generally recommends active surfaces for higher-risk people when frequent repositioning is not possible.":["u181"],"c46-operative-risk|Advocate for pressure-redistributing surfaces in the operating room.":["u182"],"c46-operative-risk|The chapter identifies higher risk with operations exceeding 3 hours, ASA scores of 3 or higher, and prone positioning.":["u183"],"c46-operative-protection|Exposed pressure areas depend on the operative position.":["u184"],"c46-operative-protection|Staff familiar with that position should protect high-risk areas with prophylactic dressings or table padding.":["u185"],"c46-pain-context-rates|Among people able to report pressure-injury pain, the chapter gives 87% reporting pain with dressing changes and 84% reporting pain at rest.":["u221"],"c46-pain-both-contexts|The chapter separately reports 42% with pain both at rest and during dressing changes.":["u222"],"c46-pain-denominator-limit|These percentages cannot describe one shared-denominator sample: 87% and 84% would require at least 71% to overlap, not 42%.":["u223"],"c46-pain-denominator-limit|The supplied summary does not identify the separate study denominators.":["u224"],"c46-pain-denominator-limit|Retain the reported findings separately; do not combine them into a single distribution.":["u225"],"c46-pain-severity-treatment|Of those with dressing-change pain, 18% described the highest, excruciating level.":["u226"],"c46-pain-severity-treatment|Only 6% of people reporting pressure-injury pain received any pain medication.":["u227"],"c46-pain-stage-gradient|Stage 3 or 4 injuries are associated with a higher proportion reporting pain, and more severe pain, than stage 2 injuries.":["u228"],"c46-dressings-chosen-by-the-wound-6|Dressing changes are the main source of pressure-injury pain.":["u112"],"c46-dressings-chosen-by-the-wound-6|Analgesia before the change, soak adherent dressings off, and consider topical lidocaine.":["u113"],"c46-colonisation-density|Pressure injuries are colonised by bacteria, commonly at ≥10^5 organisms/mL of normal skin flora.":["u229"],"c46-colonisation-density|The longer an injury remains open and unhealed, the greater the risk of biofilm.":["u230"],"c46-colonisation-density|The clinical meaning of the count depends on the tissue and wound context.":["u231"],"c46-bioburden-units|Keep the measurement attached to its sample: organisms/mL is not interchangeable with organisms per gram of tissue.":["u232"],"c46-bioburden-units|Do not turn this colonisation comparison into an automatic antibiotic threshold.":["u233"],"c46-mmp-cytokines|Persistent inflammation raises interleukin-1 and tumour necrosis factor, increasing matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs).":["u234"],"c46-mmp-cytokines|These calcium-dependent, zinc-containing enzymes degrade extracellular matrix.":["u235"],"c46-biofilm-structure|In biofilm, bacteria sit within an exopolysaccharide (EPS) matrix and are less metabolically active than free-living bacteria.":["u236"],"c46-biofilm-structure|The matrix protects against antibodies and phagocytes as well as antibiotics and antimicrobial solutions, sustaining inflammation and impaired healing.":["u237"],"c46-biofilm-recognition|Clinical studies cited by the chapter found biofilm in 60% of chronic wounds.":["u238"],"c46-biofilm-recognition|It is not visible to the naked eye; consider it in chronic wounds, especially those failing to heal, without treating suspicion as proof.":["u239"],"c46-biofilm-control|The chapter emphasises timely, adequate debridement of necrotic tissue followed by appropriate topical therapy.":["u240"],"c46-biofilm-control|Cadexomer iodine, medical-grade honey, silver and polyhexamethylene biguanide (PHMB) dressings can retard new biofilm development.":["u241"],"c46-biofilm-control|Topical treatment does not remove the need for maintenance debridement because biofilm can re-form.":["u242"],"c46-biofilm-eschar-exception|This debridement approach does not override the stable-eschar exception: dry, adherent, intact eschar without erythema or fluctuance on an ischaemic limb or the heel should not be removed.":["u243"],"c46-infection-susceptibility|Infection commonly explains a pressure injury that worsens or fails to heal.":["u244"],"c46-infection-susceptibility|Ischaemic tissue, malnutrition and poor perfusion increase susceptibility and can blunt the host response.":["u245"],"c46-infection-organisms|Common organisms include Staphylococcus aureus, Proteus mirabilis, Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Enterococcus faecalis.":["u246"],"c46-infection-organisms|Infected injuries can be reservoirs for resistant bacteria, including MRSA; colonisation alone is not the same as invasive infection.":["u247"],"c46-infection-stages-sampling|Evaluate stage 3, stage 4 and unstageable injuries for acute and chronic infection.":["u248"],"c46-infection-stages-sampling|Tissue biopsy is the reference method for wound bioburden when feasible; a quantitative wound-bed swab is an alternative.":["u249"],"c46-infection-stages-sampling|Surface or drainage cultures alone mainly describe contamination.":["u250"],"c46-levine-technique|Levine’s technique applies light pressure to the wound bed to express fluid for culture.":["u251"],"c46-levine-technique|A properly collected wound-bed sample is different from swabbing surface debris or drainage.":["u252"],"c46-systemic-antibiotic-scope|WHS guidance reserves systemic antibiotics for systemic sepsis, spreading cellulitis or underlying osteomyelitis.":["u253"],"c46-systemic-antibiotic-scope|Colonisation or a positive surface culture alone is insufficient.":["u254"],"c46-osteomyelitis-organisms|Pressure-injury-associated osteomyelitis is usually polymicrobial, commonly involving S. aureus, Enterobacteriaceae and anaerobes.":["u255"],"c46-osteomyelitis-workup|Plain radiographs may suggest osteomyelitis; the chapter recommends MRI and bone biopsy with cultures for definitive evaluation.":["u256"],"c46-osteomyelitis-confirmation|WHS guidance specifies bone biopsy for histology and culture when pressure-injury-associated osteomyelitis is suspected.":["u257"],"c46-osteomyelitis-confirmation|Imaging and exposed bone raise suspicion but do not replace that confirmation.":["u258"],"c46-osteomyelitis-treatment|Management combines debridement of infected bone with antibiotics.":["u259"],"c46-osteomyelitis-treatment|The chapter notes that infection limited to cortical bone may not require a full 6 weeks; duration depends on disease extent and the treatment plan.":["u260"],"c46-osteomyelitis-treatment|This is a limited exception, not a short-course rule for all stage 4 injuries.":["u261"],"c46-antibiotic-diarrhoea-diversion|Antibiotics can cause diarrhoea and further contaminate the wound.":["u262"],"c46-antibiotic-diarrhoea-diversion|If faecal exposure cannot be controlled, consider faecal diversion with an appropriate catheter system or a diverting colostomy.":["u263"],"c46-diversion-risk|Diversion is not automatic: WHS guidance notes that surgery may be unnecessary for healing and carries substantial risk.":["u264"],"c46-diversion-risk|Discuss alternatives, benefits and harms with the patient and caregivers.":["u265"],"c46-radical-surgery|Large infected injuries or irreversibly poor limb perfusion may lead to consideration of amputation or hemicorporectomy.":["u266"],"c46-radical-surgery|WHS guidance restricts these radical procedures to rare, extreme cases.":["u267"],"c46-surgical-complications|The chapter reports surgical complication rates as high as 50% in both younger paraplegic and nonparaplegic older patients.":["u268"],"c46-surgical-complications|Complications include dehiscence, infection, necrosis and haematoma.":["u269"],"c46-surgical-healing-recurrence|Across reported surgical series, same-site recurrence ranges from 30–70%, while 70–80% of surgically treated injuries are healed at hospital discharge.":["u270"],"c46-surgical-healing-recurrence|Discharge healing and later recurrence are different outcomes.":["u271"],"c46-surgery-new-sites-mortality|The chapter also reports new injury sites in 30% of older surgical patients and mortality ranging from nearly 50% to 68%.":["u272"],"c46-surgery-new-sites-mortality|These figures describe different outcomes; new-site injury is not same-site recurrence.":["u273"],"c46-surgical-estimate-limits|The reported surgical estimates come from different populations and outcomes; the supplied summary gives no common follow-up period.":["u274"],"c46-surgical-estimate-limits|Do not interpret them as one cohort’s discharge or 30-day mortality, or as a prediction for an individual patient.":["u275"],"c46-debridement-and-infection-1|For suspected wound infection, distinguish surface contamination from a properly collected sample: tissue biopsy or a validated quantitative wound-bed swab.":["u276"],"c46-debridement-and-infection-1|Suspected osteomyelitis calls for bone biopsy with histology and culture.":["u277"],"c46-debridement-and-infection-6|Odour, when the patient cannot be debrided — the palliative stage-4 wound the 2024 paper described — is managed by ground metronidazole applied to the wound bed; for infection needing parenteral therapy the chapter's empiric choices are cefazolin, ceftriaxone or clindamycin, vancomycin for MRSA, and metronidazole, a carbapenem or a beta-lactamase inhibitor where anaerobes are suspected.":["u116"],"c46-debridement-and-infection-6|Antiseptics belong on full-thickness wounds: cadexomer iodine, silver, honey or PHMB.":["u117"],"copy|In advanced illness the aim shifts to comfort: odour control, exudate management, pain relief at dressing changes, and repositioning only as far as the patient tolerates.":["u118"],"copy|The wounds described as skin failure or terminal ulcers are not large enough to lead to death on their own, such as would be seen with epidermolysis bullosa or Stevens-Johnson syndrome.":["u292"],"copy|The chapter states that the condition needs a different name.":["u293"],"c46-nutrition-and-what-the-evidence-actually-supports-1|The targets ch 46 gives: 30–35 kcal/kg and 1.3–1.5 g/kg of protein daily, which has been shown to improve healing significantly.":["u121"],"c46-nutrition-and-what-the-evidence-actually-supports-1|Supplements produced a relative reduction in incidence of about 25%.":["u122"],"copy|Malnutrition is a risk factor and an impediment to healing, so screen (MNA-SF) and correct energy and protein intake, with a higher protein target while a wound is open.":["u123"],"copy|What is not supported is reflexive supplementation of vitamin C or zinc in the absence of deficiency — a recurrent distractor — and neither albumin nor prealbumin is a nutritional marker in an inflamed patient; they fall as acute-phase reactants and tell you about the illness, not the diet.":["u124"],"c46-hospital-burden-premise|In the historical AHRQ comparison cited by the chapter, pressure-injury-related hospitalisations lasted 13–14 days and cost $16,755–$20,430, compared with an average stay of 5 days at approximately $10,000.":["u279"],"c46-hospital-burden-premise|These are the chapter’s historical figures, not current prices or an estimate of costs caused solely by the injury.":["u280"],"c46-quality-agenda-conclusion|This burden helps explain why pressure injuries remain on the United States national agenda for improving quality of care.":["u281"],"c46-acute-care-burden|The chapter cites the Joint Commission estimate that 2.5 million patients in US acute-care hospitals are treated for pressure injury each year.":["u219"],"c46-acute-care-burden|This is the chapter’s cited burden estimate, not a newly measured current annual rate.":["u220"],"c46-staging-npiap-2|Depth of tissue loss by pressure injury stage":["u125"],"copy|Exam pearl":["u126","u127"]}}
